GCP/Azure 90-day retention — measured 2026-08-08
================================================

Claim under test (NEXT_PLAN "승인 필요", STATUS Risk 2):

  "GCP/Azure는 90일 보관을 집행하지 않는다. 둘 다 `ttl`을 쓰지만 스토어가 안 켜져
   있어 무기한 남는다. 켜는 건 실 데이터 삭제 → 승인 필요."

The first half is true and is a code fact. The second half — "켜는 건 실 데이터
삭제" — was never measured, and it is what made this an approval item for nine days.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. GCP — the Firestore incident store does not exist
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Store per code: `INCIDENT_COLLECTION` default `incident-history`, Firestore
(`src/agents/operations/gcp/{analyzer,executor}.py`).

platform-agent's GCP project is `project-ec7809f7-0fb5-45d4-b6d` (the billing map:
it is the only project with an open billing account, and the 2026-07 GKE cost
incident happened there).

  $ gcloud firestore databases list --project=project-ec7809f7-0fb5-45d4-b6d
  ERROR: Cloud Firestore API has not been used in project
         project-ec7809f7-0fb5-45d4-b6d before or it is disabled.

  $ gcloud firestore fields ttls list --database="(default)" \
        --project=project-ec7809f7-0fb5-45d4-b6d
  ERROR: PERMISSION_DENIED: Cloud Firestore API has not been used ...

The API has never been enabled, so there is no database, so there is no
`incident-history` collection and no document in it.

Swept the other reachable projects rather than trusting one:

  claude-study-501117          -> one database `coffee-menu` (unrelated), TTL
                                  fields list is EMPTY (no TTL policy)
  gen-lang-client-0123174100   -> Firestore API not enabled
  gen-lang-client-0983122780   -> Firestore API not enabled

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Azure — the Cosmos incident container does not exist
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Store per code: `AZURE_COSMOS_DATABASE` default `platform-agent`,
`AZURE_INCIDENT_CONTAINER` default `incident-history`
(`src/agents/operations/azure/{analyzer,executor,decision}.py`).

  $ az account list
  one subscription: "Azure subscription 1" (23842f7d-...)

  $ az cosmosdb list
  one account: cosmos-roadpilot / rg-roadpilot

  $ az cosmosdb sql database list --account-name cosmos-roadpilot \
        --resource-group rg-roadpilot
  ["roadpilot"]

There is no `platform-agent` database and therefore no `incident-history`
container. Nothing to expire, nothing to delete.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. What this changes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The approval was framed as "enabling retention deletes real data". It does not,
because there is no data and no store — and you cannot set `DefaultTimeToLive` on
a container that does not exist or a Firestore TTL policy on a database that was
never created.

So the binding constraint is the opposite of the recorded one: enabling retention
requires PROVISIONING Firestore and Cosmos first, which is billable and belongs
with Phase 4 (managed adapters) — not a destructive-approval item at all.

The code-level gap stays real and stays worth keeping written down: if those
stores are ever provisioned, `ttl` is written but nothing expires it, and the
"90일 보관" claim would be false the moment there is data to be wrong about.

Same shape as D40: a blocker whose recorded reason was true-sounding, repeated
across documents, and not the one actually binding. Nobody had run the query.

